------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Upstream origin and attribution
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Rizx Player is an independent hard fork / clean-room reimplementation of the
business logic of Nuclear, the open-source desktop music player:

    Nuclear — https://github.com/nukeop/nuclear
    Copyright (C) nukeop and the Nuclear contributors
    Licensed under the GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 (AGPL-3.0)

Rizx re-implements Nuclear's domain logic (providers, queue, two-phase stream
resolution, playback, favorites, playlists) on native Android (Kotlin, Jetpack
Compose, Media3/ExoPlayer, Room, DataStore). It is NOT a port of Nuclear's
React/Tauri desktop UI, is not affiliated with or endorsed by the Nuclear
project, and does not upstream patches (upstream does not accept contributions).

As a derived work, Rizx Player carries the full AGPL-3.0 obligations
independently: the license text, this attribution, the Corresponding Source, and
a log of major modifications are preserved and distributed with every build.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Corresponding Source (AGPL §6)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The complete Corresponding Source for each distributed build is published in this
project's public Git repository, tagged to match the build's versionName /
versionCode. The in-app About screen links to it. Build configuration required to
reproduce the build (Gradle) is included; signing keys and secrets are not.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
AGPL §13 (remote network interaction) — assessment
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The MVP is a standalone client with no backend or server component operated by
the authors, so §13 is not triggered. If a server/remote component is ever added
(hosted backend, remote-control API, cast/MPD server, etc.), §13 activates and
that component must offer its Corresponding Source to remote users. See
docs/LICENSING.md.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Major modifications relative to upstream Nuclear
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- Complete native Android reimplementation (Kotlin / Jetpack Compose / Material 3
  / Navigation Compose) replacing the React/Redux/Electron(Tauri) desktop UI.
- Media3 / ExoPlayer + MediaSessionService background playback replacing the
  Web Audio / MSE / hls.js web streaming stack.
- Room + DataStore persistence replacing the desktop store.
- Provider architecture (metadata + streaming providers, registry, two-phase
  ephemeral stream resolution) re-modeled from upstream packages/model and
  plugin-sdk; first real provider integration uses the public iTunes Search API.
- Sandboxed QuickJS plugin runtime that downloads and runs real Nuclear plugins
  (network-only sandbox: no filesystem, no Android APIs, no WebView), with
  per-plugin timeouts and crash quarantine. This supersedes the earlier decision
  to ship no plugin runtime at all.
- Ambient music recognition ("Audio ID"), which upstream Nuclear does not have:
  on-device acoustic fingerprinting of microphone audio, a keyless lookup against
  an unofficial Shazam-compatible endpoint, and a multi-provider resolver that
  locates the identified recording in this app's own catalogue by ISRC or Apple
  id before falling back to a scored search. The fingerprint algorithm is a
  Kotlin reimplementation of the format documented by SongRec (GPL-3.0,
  https://github.com/marin-m/SongRec); no SongRec code is bundled or linked, and
  no Shazam/ShazamKit SDK is used. Captured audio is processed in memory and
  discarded — never stored, never transmitted.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Bundled third-party components
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Open-source libraries bundled in the app are listed with their licenses in
docs/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md and surfaced in-app via About -> Open-source
licenses. Bundled fonts are licensed under the SIL Open Font License 1.1 (see
Proyecto/README.md). Search results and 30-second audio previews are fetched at
runtime from the iTunes Search API (Apple Inc.) under Apple's terms; no Apple
code or assets are bundled.
